REAL FIX (class-wide): skip gnulib's re_compile_pattern *run* test, which hangs
forever on arm64 Haiku.

Symptom: `haikuporter -y tar` never finishes. The build log stops dead at

    checking for working re_compile_pattern...

and stays there. Nothing fails, nothing times out, there is no error to grep
for -- the build simply sits. `ps` in the guest shows why:

    /bin/sh ./configure --prefix=/packages/tar-1.35-2/.self --sysco   64352
    /bin/sh ./configure --prefix=/packages/tar-1.35-2/.self --sysco   69516
    ./conftest                                                       69517

`./conftest` is wedged, and two log-line samples 30 s apart both read 542 lines.

That check is gnulib's `AC_RUN_IFELSE` for the regex module: it *compiles and
runs* a program against the system's `re_compile_pattern`. Something in Haiku's
arm64 regex implementation does not return, so configure blocks on a child that
never exits.

## Why nothing hit this until now

Every gnulib-heavy tool already in the image -- grep, sed, findutils, gawk,
coreutils -- was **cross**-compiled on the metal as a `_bootstrap` package. Read
the configure source (tar-1.35/configure:40985):

    *)      gl_cv_func_re_compile_pattern_working="$gl_cross_guess_normal" ;;

so when cross compiling autoconf cannot run the test and substitutes a guess. The
test only actually executes on a **native** build. This is therefore not a tar
bug: it will hang every native gnulib-based port, and it is the first thing to
suspect whenever a native build goes quiet rather than failing.

## The fix

`--with-included-regex` is a documented gnulib configure option that
short-circuits the whole thing. From the same configure
(tar-1.35/configure:40963):

      case $with_included_regex in #(
      yes|no) ac_use_included_regex=$with_included_regex
    ...
      *)
        # ... following run test ...
        { printf "%s\n" "... checking for working re_compile_pattern" >&5

The `checking for working re_compile_pattern` block lives only in the `*)`
default branch, so passing an explicit yes/no never reaches it. No cache-variable
seeding, no timeout wrapper, no patched conftest.

`--with-included-regex` (yes) rather than `--without-included-regex` is the
conservative choice: gnulib then builds its own regex.c instead of trusting the
system one. Since the probe *hangs* we have no evidence that Haiku's
`re_compile_pattern` is correct, and asserting "no" would be claiming a result
the test never produced.

Follow-up worth filing separately: the hang itself is a genuine arm64 Haiku
defect in libroot's regex, not just a build-system annoyance. This patch routes
around it; it does not fix it.

Applies to: input-source-packages/develop/sources/tar-1.35-2/tar-1.35.recipe
Note the comment goes *above* runConfigure, not among its arguments: the argument
list is one backslash-continued command, so a `#` line inside it would terminate
the command and drop everything after it.

--- a/tar-1.35.recipe
+++ b/tar-1.35.recipe
@@ -/+ BUILD()
+	# --with-included-regex: gnulib's "checking for working re_compile_pattern"
+	# is an AC_RUN_IFELSE whose conftest never returns on arm64 Haiku, so
+	# configure hangs with no error at all. Passing an explicit yes/no skips
+	# that run test entirely (configure:40963). yes is chosen because the probe
+	# never produced a result, so claiming the system regex works would be
+	# unfounded. Cross-built _bootstrap packages never hit this: cross
+	# compiling substitutes $gl_cross_guess_normal instead of running the test.
 	runConfigure --omit-dirs binDir ./configure --bindir=$commandBinDir \
 		--disable-nls \
 		--disable-gcc-warnings \
+		--with-included-regex \
 		LDFLAGS="-lnetwork" CFLAGS="-D_BSD_SOURCE" \
 		FORCE_UNSAFE_CONFIGURE=1 \
 		$yearArg
